<%# Every proposal the backfill left, in one place. #520's scope, reversed deliberately.

    ## Why this screen exists at all

    #520 put the question on the workout record and that is still where it belongs for a
    session finished ten minutes ago: the lifter is already looking at it, and the proposal
    is about the thing they just did. The backfill breaks the assumption underneath that,
    which is that proposals arrive one at a time. A first run over a few years can leave
    eighty of them, and answering eighty from the record pages means finding eighty days in
    a list that is ordered by date and says nothing about which of them have a question
    waiting. That is a feature nobody gets to the end of, and a half-answered backfill is
    worse than none: the proposals that were never looked at sit there looking answered.

    ## What it is not

    It is not a second way to match. The two forms below post to the same two routes the
    record page posts to, which run the same `confirm` and `dismiss` -- so a yes here and a
    yes there are the same write, and there is one place where a match is made.

    The wording and the evidence are shared with the record page in the only way that
    matters: `because` composes the sentence, once, in lib. What is written out again here
    is the markup around it, because the two screens answer different questions -- that one
    asks "was this activity this session" about the session you are looking at, and this one
    asks "which of these eighty are right" -- and forcing one template to do both would make
    a partial with a mode flag in it.

    ## Nothing here is a live fetch

    The route reads rows the backfill already wrote. Opening this page asks Withings nothing,
    which is what keeps it safe to leave open and reload. %>
<div class="px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Matches from your watch</h1>
  <%- if @proposals.empty? %>
    <%# **Absent is absent here.** The record page tells a session minutes old to check back
        in a minute, because the watch's upload really is seconds to minutes behind. This
        page is about history, where there is no upload on its way, so it says there is
        nothing waiting and does not promise anything is coming. %>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
      Nothing is waiting for an answer. Proposals appear here after a backfill runs, and
      disappear as you answer them &mdash; whether you answer them here or on the session&rsquo;s
      own record.
    </p>
  <%- else %>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <%# The verb agrees with the count as well as the noun. "1 session ... overlap" was the
          state this shipped in: the noun was pluralised and the verb was not, so the one case
          a lifter reaches by answering all but the last question read as broken English at
          exactly the moment the page was nearly done with them. %>
      <%= @proposals.length %> session<%= 's' unless @proposals.length == 1 %> from your history
      overlap<%= 's' if @proposals.length == 1 %> something your watch recorded. Each one is a
      guess &mdash; say which are right.
    </p>
    <%# That the other door exists, said on this side too. #534.

        The record page now says how many are waiting here; this says the questions can be
        answered there. Without both halves the two screens read as two chores that happen to
        look alike, and a lifter who had answered one on a record would reasonably wonder
        whether they had also answered it here.

        It is worth saying on this side in particular because of what the row below offers:
        the date is a link to the session, and it is there because "18:02 to 18:50 on the 14th
        of March" is only recognisable to somebody who can go and look at what was lifted. A
        lifter who follows it to check lands on a page with the very same question on it, and
        this sentence is what stops that being a surprise -- answering it there is not
        escaping this list, it is finishing one row of it. %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-700">
      Each one can be answered on the session&rsquo;s own record instead &mdash; follow the date
      to check what was lifted and the question is there too. It is the same answer either way.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-6 divide-y divide-gray-200">
      <%- @proposals.each do |proposal| %>
        <li class="py-4">
          <%# The session first and as a link, because the question cannot be answered from
              the times alone: "the 14th of March, 18:02 to 18:50" is only recognisable to
              somebody who can go and look at what was lifted that day. %>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">
            <a href="/workouts/<%= proposal[:workout][:id] %>" class="hover:underline">
              <%= proposal[:workout][:date].strftime('%b %d, %Y') %>
            </a>
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-700">
            Withings recorded an activity
            <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: proposal[:activity][:started_at] }) %>
            to
            <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: proposal[:activity][:ended_at] }) %>.
            Was that this session?
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-600">It <%= proposal[:because] %>.</p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <%# `back` is what returns the lifter here rather than to the record they have
                not asked to see. It names a destination the route already knows rather than
                carrying a URL, so a forged form cannot redirect anybody anywhere. %>
            <form method="post" action="/workouts/<%= proposal[:workout][:id] %>/withings/match">
              <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{proposal[:workout][:id]}/withings/match") %>
              <input type="hidden" name="activity" value="<%= proposal[:activity][:external_id] %>">
              <input type="hidden" name="back" value="proposals">
              <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 bg-lime-500 px-3 text-sm font-semibold
                                            text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Yes</button>
            </form>
            <form method="post" action="/workouts/<%= proposal[:workout][:id] %>/withings/dismiss">
              <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{proposal[:workout][:id]}/withings/dismiss") %>
              <input type="hidden" name="activity" value="<%= proposal[:activity][:external_id] %>">
              <input type="hidden" name="back" value="proposals">
              <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 border border-gray-300 bg-white px-3
                                            text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-50">No</button>
            </form>
          </div>
        </li>
      <%- end %>
    </ul>
  <%- end %>
</div>
